#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""biteprank — the prank you send, not the prank you run.

Nothing here touches your friend's machine. It cannot: a web page in a browser
tab has no files, no shell, no persistence. What it does have is a full screen,
a speaker, and — this is the part that lands — a small pile of things about the
visitor that are genuinely true. Their city. Their carrier. Their exact battery
percentage, ticking down as they watch. When a page reads your real numbers back
to you the fake numbers beside them stop feeling fake, and that is the entire
trick. No exploit, just theatre with two honest sentences in it.

So the toy is a generator, not a runner. You pick a scene, it forges one
self-contained .html file — every byte inlined, sound synthesised by WebAudio,
no external request except the one geo lookup — and then it puts that file
somewhere your friend can reach: saved to disk to send by hand, served over your
LAN, pushed through a cloudflared tunnel for a public link, or committed to a
GitHub Pages repo for a link that outlives this terminal.

Lines it does not cross, deliberately: it never asks for a password or a login,
never collects anything anywhere (the intel is read in their tab and dies with
it), never phones home, and ESC always ends the show immediately. Every scene
resolves to the same reveal — a big GOTCHA, then a plain sentence telling them
nothing happened and nothing was taken. A prank they can't get out of, or can't
tell was a prank afterwards, is just being mean to someone.

Settings arrive from the hub as TOY_* env vars (see config.def). Defaults are
repeated here so the toy still runs standalone, outside the hub.
"""

import hashlib
import html
import json
import os
